\begin{abstract}
We develop a physically active extension of the Tensor Polarizable Vacuum (TPV)
interpretation of general relativity in which the vacuum is identified with spacetime
itself and acquires a matter-gated tensor constitutive response.  The leading operator is
constructed from the magnetic part of the Weyl tensor measured in the local matter
frame,
\begin{equation*}
 \Delta S =
 \frac{\mpl^2}{2}\frac{\alpha_B}{\Lambda_Q^2}
 \int \dd^4x\sqrt{-g}\,
 \Gamma\, B_{\mu\nu}B^{\mu\nu}.
\end{equation*}
The matter four-velocity is not an independent aether field but is supplied by the
covariant perfect-fluid sector.  The theory is treated as an effective field theory (EFT)
and defined perturbatively by reduction of order.  To first order in
$\alpha_B/\Lambda_Q^2$, the unknown corrections are acted on by the ordinary
linearized Einstein--fluid operator; higher-derivative terms enter only as sources
evaluated on the general-relativistic solution.  Hence the retained low-energy branch
requires no additional initial data beyond general relativity plus the original matter
degrees of freedom.

All static configurations aligned with the matter rest frame satisfy
$B_{\mu\nu}=0$.  Consequently every static general-relativistic perfect-fluid solution
is also an exact TPV solution, and the zero-frequency gravitoelectric tidal response is
unchanged.  Rotation activates the new constitutive sector.  In the Hartle slow-rotation
limit we derive a positive-definite first-order correction to the stellar moment of
inertia.  For $\alpha_B>0$ and a nonnegative gate, the correction obeys
$\Delta I>0$ analytically.  An illustrative $1.4\,M_\odot$ tabulated SLY4 benchmark
with $R=11.7240\,{\rm km}$ and $I_{\rm GR}=101.4204\,{\rm km^3}$ gives
$\Delta I/I_{\rm GR}=7.54\times10^{-4}$ at $\alpha_B=0.05$ for the fiducial gate and
$\Lambda_Q$ used here.  The theory therefore leaves the static mass--radius and
gravitoelectric tidal sectors unchanged while modifying the gravitomagnetic response
of rotating matter.  We discuss the EFT domain, limitations, and the outstanding
second-order rotational quadrupole calculation.
\end{abstract}

